Sūta said:
Brahmā Prajāpati, the Lord, consecrated the Sun
to the lordship of the planets, and Soma to that of the constellations and herbs;

Sūta said:
Creation of the ancients is said to proceed from volition, sight, and touch;
after Dakṣa, the son of Pracetas, creation arose from sexual union.

Sūta said:
The brother of Hiraṇyakaśipu was known as Hiraṇyākṣa;
he was of old the father of Andhaka, the lord of the Asuras, equal to the Finisher of Death.

Sūta said:
The eight-syllable [mantra], O best of brahmins, is 'Homage to Nārāyaṇa';
and the twelve-syllable mantra of the Supreme Self is supreme.

"Oṃ bhūḥ brahma hṛdayāya; oṃ bhuvaḥ viṣṇuśirase; oṃ svaḥ rudraśikhāyai; oṃ bhūr bhuvaḥ svaḥ jvālāmālinīśikhāyai; oṃ mahaḥ maheśvarāya kavacāya; oṃ janaḥ śivāya netrebhyaḥ;
oṃ tapaḥ tāpakāya astrāya phaṭ." Thus the various solar mantras are declared;
with these vessels, such as horns, he should consecrate himself.

proceeding along the path purified by the water offering and stepping on sandals,
he should perform the hand-placement and body-placement as before;
taking the sacred water according to rule and entering the place of worship,

“Oṃ bhūḥ, oṃ bhuvaḥ, oṃ svaḥ, oṃ mahaḥ, oṃ janaḥ, oṃ tapaḥ, oṃ satyam, oṃ ṛtam, oṃ brahman”—the formula consisting of nine syllables is proclaimed as the Bāṣkala.
Because it does not decay in this world, it is called the syllable;
the syllable is declared to be truth, beginning with the praṇava and ending with “homage”.

one should contemplate the destroyer of mundane existence having the form of a pure lamp-flame;
and one should worship the god Sadāśiva in a liṅga or on an altar.

Thus ends the twenty-third chapter in the Uttarabhāga of the glorious Liṅgamahāpurāṇa.

Sanatkumāra said:
Now [I will describe] another subtle mountain, of little material yet of great fruit;
at a time when materials are merely minimal, a middling [procedure] is prescribed.

One should also make the lords of obstacles with the previously stated measure of gold;
having worshipped them according to rule with perfumes, flowers, and the like, in order—

Thus ends the thirty-first chapter in the Uttarabhāga of the glorious Liṅga Mahāpurāṇa.

Sanatkumāra said:
The recitation, fire oblation, worship, giving, consecration, and other rites are as before;
I shall describe in brief the gift of the golden earth.

And one should worship Śaṅkara, Śiva, with a thousand jars and the like;
the excellent gift of the golden earth has been proclaimed in this Purāṇa of the Liṅga.

Thus ends the thirty-second chapter, entitled "The Gift of the Golden Earth", in the Uttarabhāga of the glorious Liṅga Mahāpurāṇa.

Sanatkumāra said:
Now I shall proclaim another excellent [gift], the wish-granting tree;
having made it with a hundred niṣkas, endowed with all its branches,

Sanatkumāra said:
I shall proclaim the gift of Gaṇeśeśa in the previously mentioned pavilion;
having worshipped the god of the gods of gods, surrounded by the world protectors,

Therefore, abandoning all else, one should install the imperishable liṅga;
if it is installed with effort, everything is installed; [if] it is worshipped, [everything] is worshipped.

Thus ends the forty-sixth chapter in the Uttarabhāga of the glorious Liṅgamahāpurāṇa.

And what is the divine knowledge that bestows liberation, by which creatures are freed?
